<section id="why" class="alt">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Why enzymes are important</h2>
    <p class="lead">Enzymes make the chemical reactions needed for life happen quickly enough and under the mild conditions inside living cells.</p>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card"><div class="ico"><svg class="i"><use href="#bolt"/></svg></div><h3>They speed up reactions</h3><p>Enzymes lower the activation energy needed for a reaction. This means reactions can happen faster without needing high temperatures.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ico pop"><svg class="i"><use href="#grain"/></svg></div><h3>They help release energy</h3><p>Digestive enzymes break large food molecules into smaller nutrients. Cells can then use these nutrients in chemical reactions, including cellular respiration, to release energy.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ico"><svg class="i"><use href="#shield"/></svg></div><h3>They protect cells</h3><p>Enzymes such as catalase break down harmful substances made inside cells. Other enzymes help important processes, such as copying DNA, take place.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card" style="margin-top:22px">
      <h3>Why this matters</h3>
      <p>Without enzymes, many reactions in the body would happen too slowly to keep us alive. Enzymes are specific: each active site fits certain substrates, and the enzyme can be used again after the reaction.</p>
    </div>
    <h3 style="margin-top:1.6em">Example: amylase in saliva</h3>
    <p>Amylase speeds up the breakdown of starch into smaller sugars, such as maltose. This is one of the first steps in digesting carbohydrates.</p>
    <div class="formula">Starch + water <span style="font-size:.8em">— amylase →</span> smaller sugars</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="examples">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Enzymes we learned about</h2>
    <p class="lead">Three digestive enzymes, each with its own job.</p>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card"><div class="ico"><svg class="i"><use href="#drop"/></svg></div><h3>Lipase</h3><p>Breaks down fats into fatty acids and glycerol.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ico pop"><svg class="i"><use href="#grain"/></svg></div><h3>Amylase</h3><p>Breaks down starch into smaller sugars.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ico"><svg class="i"><use href="#scissors"/></svg></div><h3>Protease</h3><p>Breaks down proteins into smaller peptides and amino acids.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>


<section id="deep" class="alt">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Three enzymes in more detail</h2>
    <p class="lead">These enzymes have different jobs in digestion and protection.</p>

    <article class="feature" id="pepsin">
      <div class="ico"><svg class="i" style="width:36px;height:36px"><use href="#stomach"/></svg></div>
      <div>
        <h3>Pepsin</h3>
        <p>Pepsin is a digestive enzyme found in the stomach. It breaks large protein molecules into smaller pieces called peptides, making them easier for the body to digest further.</p>
        <p>It works best in the acidic conditions of the stomach. Pepsin is first made in an inactive form and becomes active when stomach acid is present. A protective mucus layer containing bicarbonate helps stop the stomach lining from being damaged by the acid and pepsin.</p>
        
    <svg class="protein-figure" viewBox="0 0 300 175" role="img" aria-label="Simplified illustration of the folded pepsin protein">
      <path d="M55 92 C35 66 58 36 88 44 C100 16 137 24 150 44 C175 22 209 38 205 66 C239 72 246 106 220 122 C208 150 174 146 155 128 C131 151 98 140 96 118 C70 128 46 112 55 92Z" fill="#d7d9c5" stroke="#71815c" stroke-width="4"/>
      <path class="strand" d="M72 83 C89 56 105 66 116 86 S143 111 154 86 S184 57 202 79 M78 109 C98 91 115 104 128 121 S160 137 176 113 S203 96 222 107"
        fill="none" stroke="#71815c" stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round"/>
      <path d="M125 72 Q147 55 171 72 Q181 84 166 98 Q146 105 129 91Z" fill="#d99a62" stroke="#203b36" stroke-width="2"/>
      <path d="M166 77 L224 45" stroke="#203b36" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
      <text x="226" y="43" font-size="11" fill="#203b36" font-family="Nunito,sans-serif">Active site</text>
      <text x="150" y="166" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#526d66" font-family="Nunito,sans-serif">Simplified protein structure</text>
    </svg>
        <p class="protein-caption">The active site is the part where the substrate binds.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="feature" id="catalase">
      <div class="ico pop"><svg class="i" style="width:36px;height:36px"><use href="#heart"/></svg></div>
      <div>
        <h3>Catalase</h3>
        <p>Catalase is found in almost all living cells, including liver cells. Its job is to break down hydrogen peroxide (H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>), a harmful substance that can form during reactions inside cells.</p>
        <p>It converts hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen. This helps protect cells from damage. Catalase works extremely quickly: one enzyme molecule can break down millions of hydrogen peroxide molecules in a second.</p>
        <div class="formula" style="margin-top:.6em">2H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> → 2H<sub>2</sub>O + O<sub>2</sub></div>
        
    <svg class="protein-figure" viewBox="0 0 300 175" role="img" aria-label="Simplified illustration of the folded catalase protein">
      <path d="M54 89 C39 61 67 34 94 46 C108 18 140 26 151 45 C178 24 210 40 208 68 C240 78 241 108 218 123 C203 148 172 145 154 128 C130 151 96 140 94 117 C69 126 45 111 54 89Z" fill="#c8ddcf" stroke="#477d70" stroke-width="4"/>
      <path class="strand" d="M72 83 C89 56 105 66 116 86 S143 111 154 86 S184 57 202 79 M78 109 C98 91 115 104 128 121 S160 137 176 113 S203 96 222 107"
